 cash advantage ($4.3M vs $1.0M)"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Republicans, but a favorable environment, Wittman's modest incumbency edge, and a fresh independent poll showing Taylor ahead point to a genuine tossup with a slight Democratic tilt. Markets and several raters still lean Republican, keeping the margin narrow.", "watch": "Any additional independent polling or a shift in the national environment could move this across the lean line in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 1st District pairs a well-funded incumbent, Rob Wittman, against Henrico-area prosecutor Shannon Taylor in a seat that leans Republican by about three points but sits in a strongly Democratic national environment. Expert ratings are split: several call it a Tossup, others Lean R, and one rates it a Democratic flip. Prediction markets give Wittman a modest edge, pricing Taylor around 46 percent.\n\nPolling is thin but recent. A late-September Washington Post/SSPG survey of likely voters shows Taylor up four points, while a Democratic-sponsored internal poll had Wittman ahead by one. Wittman holds a large financial advantage and has faced some outside opposition spending. The race is a genuine coin flip with a slight Democratic tilt, and it is widely viewed as a national bellwether.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4341, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4371, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3736, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3951, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4096, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4532, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.638, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6374, "marg